ge kind of thing versus you  know we need an institutional and so  organization because the life of humans  by themselves is nasty brutish and short  sometimes these debates get kind of  polarized according to modern political  viewpoints and lose a lot of I think  what's really interesting is the nuance  but and below that so in terms of  hunter-gatherers there is a lot we can  learn actually from the way they  organized their communities together but  there's also a lot we can learn from and  the imbalances that they created so  something that most people don't know is  that it was actually while humans were  hunter-gatherers tens even hundreds of  thousands of years ago that we actually  created and some of the greatest mass  extinctions in all of the Earth's  history so you know when and when humans  first left Africa and went to Asia and  then to Australia and then went across  Siberia to the Americas they just  thought well nature is so bountiful all  these big mammals out there and of  course those melons had no instinctive  fear of humans the humans had this power  of having be able to develop tools so  over a number of generations they led to  these mass extinctions about  three-quarters of what's called the  megafauna of North and South America  went extinct and over ninety percent of  Australia's megafauna went extinct and I  think the lesson we can learn from that  is the how humans can create these  imbalances and with the natural world  oftentimes without even being aware of  it when you talk about imbalances I mean  right now it's hard to avoid having a  conversation about climate change and in  the book of course you're not arguing we  shouldn't worry about climate change but  you do talk about how if we fix climate  change whatever form that solution  ultimately takes that humanity will  create some new existential threat can  you talk about why you feel so strongly  about that yes and I do think that is so  important for us to understand because I  think we need  to recognize the climate change is  really more of a symptom of what's wrong  with our our place in the in the world  in our relationship with the natural  world and relationship with ourselves  and rather than of course of something  so it's a little bit like the canary in  the mind that is the early sign that  there are some really deep imbalances  and I think if you just look at a deeper  place you see that our whole and global  system encourages us to consume at this  growing rate and and everything about  our values is based on this notion the  consumption and growth and consumption  is necessary for human flourishing and I  think the opposite in fact is true and  that right now we're on a an increase  accelerating course towards a precipice  that is not just in relation to climate  change but in relation to the ways in  which we we are causing now our own  sixth mass extinction of species and  we're utilizing resources at a  completely unsustainable rate including  things like freshwater and ocean fish  and leading to significant deforestation  desertification around the world all  these kind of issues are connected with  each other and if we try to just solve  one issue alone like climate change what  we're really doing is just kind of  kicking the can down the road until a  next bigger issue hits us so what needs  to change more systemically yeah I think  that what we need to do is shift some of  the underlying values that lead us as a  global civilization to this behavior of  ever-increasing consumption like into  them and if you think of our wealth  system like some kind of software and  it's as though we keep getting bigger  and bigger software glitches and we keep  trying to fix those glitches but what we  really need to do is look at the  operating system underneath and that  operating system in our current world  view our current way of finding meaning  has to do with things like and  consumerism  and this desire for growth in  possessions and for growth and status in  terms of what we consume as being what  leads to a sense of meaning in life  rather than other ways of finding  meaning that are more sustainable well  to take your software analogy it seemed  if we tweak it set slightly it seems  that the world approach to dealing with  issues calamities is sort of that of  creating a software patch which prevents  the entire program from ceasing to  function but if you keep patching the  same piece of outdated software  eventually it will no longer work with  the hardware and it maybe there's  something there to that analogy in terms  of the human approach to solving  problems but at the same time when  you're talking about billions of humans  dealing with complex interrelated  problems isn't it just from a practical  level very difficult to get worldwide  consensus on any problem yes I think  it's very difficult to get worldwide  consensus on any problem and yet what  we've seen is there are there are  moments in history when something like  that has happened so if you just think  back a couple of hundred years ago and  slavery was considered to be as  essential to and the sort of an optimal  working of the modern world at that time  as global capitalism is considered now  but at what as a certain level of  cultural evolution occurred something  shifted in the sense of what is right  what we're somewhere meaning arises and  when morality arises and so now even  though of course and there are incidents  still of slavery happening in the world  virtually everyone in the world agrees  that that's a bad thing and that should  be illegal so there are these shifts  that happen and in spite of what seems  to be like overwhelming odds and to me  that is a source of hope even as we look  at some of the really quite and yeah  quite terrifying directions we're going  in right now well there are numerous  futurists and scientists that believe  we're at a sort of inflection point  right now so  a Kurtz file f