 I get so afraid that  these people oftentimes are just  focusing on the a leaps  it within the whole human race at  seven-and-a-half billion of us as the  people who get the benefits of that so  there's a scenario that I call techno  splits where basically and the the  people who are currently the and the  elite in the world may be the wealthiest  half billion or so get to enjoy genetic  enhancement neural interfaces and maybe  do get to live for hundreds of years or  whatever but meanwhile billions of other  human beings are suffering essentially a  collapse from climate change and all  these other environmental disasters that  are hitting us I think that's a very  important issue for us to be aware of  whenever we hear are some of these kind  of hopeful optimistic scenarios because  if you're like me your sense of being a  human being is not just being this  individual and rejecting our sense of  connection with others but there's a  really powerful sense of us being a  human family and we need to be really  focused on the idea that technological  advance is there for all of humanity not  just for an elite year well the book is  fascinating it's called the patterning  instinct the cultural history of  humanity search for meaning we've been  speaking with the book's author Jeremy  Lenz  Jeremy thank you so much for talking to  me about this today yeah thank you very  much David

